# Track User Logins on Network Machines

This project is part of the **Google IT Automation with Python Professional Certificate** (Crash Course on Python - Module 6). It provides a Python solution to process an event log containing user logins and logouts across various servers and workstations, ultimately generating a report of all users currently active on each machine.

## Project Overview

The objective is to process a list of system events to determine which users are currently logged into which machines. The script processes each log entry, tracks user sessions dynamically, and prints out a clean summary report.

### Key Logic & Features
- **Event Sorting:** Events are sorted automatically by chronological timestamp to ensure tracking accuracy.
- **Edge Case Handling:** Includes safe tracking methods (`set.discard()`) to elegantly handle unexpected logout logs where a user might log out without a recorded login event, preventing runtime key or set errors.
- **Dynamic Set Tracking:** Utilizes Python `set` structures per machine to ensure users are stored uniquely and removed effortlessly upon logging out.

---

## Code Structure

The project consists of three main utility functions and an `Event` data model:

1. **`get_event_date(event)`**: A helper function used as a sorting key to sort events chronologically.
2. **`current_users(events)`**: Processes the collection of events, building a dictionary where keys are machine names and values are sets of active users.
3. **`generate_report(machines)`**: Iterates over the tracked machines and prints a formatted report listing all currently logged-in users.
4. **`Event` Class**: A lightweight data object that initializes event attributes (`date`, `type`, `machine`, `user`).

